## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** Thread.Abort patterns that all need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` + `Stop()` calls `Abort()` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **ThreadAbortException as control flow** | `catch` block rolls back transaction | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort to continue** | Prevents rethrow so loop continues | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check; new CTS per item |

### Why it breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it will **never** stop your thread. `Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws. Your `Stop()` method becomes a no-op after the 2-second sleep.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Per-item CTS linked to the parent, so cancelling the parent
            // also cancels the current item — replacing Thread.Abort's
            // ability to interrupt mid-item.
            using var itemCts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(token);
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(itemCts.Token);  // pass token so blocking unblocks
                ProcessItem(item, itemCts.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Shutdown requested — clean up and exit the loop
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Individual item cancelled but parent still alive —
                // replaces the old ResetAbort "continue the loop" pattern
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();

        // Give the thread time to finish cooperatively
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | What to do |
|---------|------------|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | It must accept a `CancellationToken` and honor it. If it wraps a `BlockingCollection`, use `Take(token)`. If it calls `WaitHandle.WaitOne()`, switch to `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { handle, token.WaitHandle })`. Without this, `Stop()` will hang. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` inside `ProcessItem`** | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` — `Thread.Sleep` ignores cancellation. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Both the parent `_cts` and per-item linked sources must be disposed to avoid timer/callback leaks. The `using` and `Dispose()` above handle this. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter distinguishes shutdown from per-item cancellation. Without it, you'd accidentally swallow shutdown signals. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` between expensive operations if responsiveness matters. |

### Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort`, `ThreadAbortException`, or `ResetAbort` references
- ✅ `CancellationToken` flows through blocking calls
- ✅ Cleanup logic (rollback) preserved in `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
- ✅ `FlushBuffers()` still runs via `finally`
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` properly disposed
- ✅ `Thread.Sleep(2000)` replaced with `Thread.Join(timeout)`
